<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artibonite</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Artibonite River, Artibonite Valley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Artibonite is the longest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> and flowing about 320 km west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> to the Gulf of Gonâve. Its broad valley is Haiti's main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing region, and it gives its name to the Artibonite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, whose capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonaives">Gonaïves</a>. Dessalines built his fortified capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marchand-dessalines">Marchand</a> in the valley, and the river later formed the frontier between Christophe's north and Pétion's south.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artibonite.jpg" alt="Artibonite" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kendra Helmer, USAID · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artibonite_River_in_Haiti_(2010).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
